{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for 96d82e16ab8337050031a3dcd1f0e449ea409900, tree 61e30378b34b1813e8678d5d508518fcb8c3082e. Complete one-file review; findings below are against notes/2026-10-17-grow-operation-application-design.md.\n\nF1 (P2), lines 374–376: name the destination features this design actually depends on. S2 plus M1 is not enough for the described package. The reviewed applications note, section 7, explicitly assigns declared projections, the generic interface, several scopes per package, bounded capabilities and presentation-only updates to M2. This note uses all of those, including present at 337–338. State which slices are only a simulation/sketch after M1 and which await M2 or a separately designed prerequisite; do not silently pull M2 into S2/M1. Keep this as a non-commissioning design after the journey exit test. Cite the exact milestone section and the reviewed Main baseline.\n\nF2 (P2), lines 80–98, 157–160 and 257–263: make the actuator authority and execution chain coherent. The plain declared ActType.grant at Main is one fixed string, not an action chosen by a device-kind guard. The table currently gives the same set-device act grow.climate for fans/vents and grow.heat for heaters. Prefer distinct declared acts or spell out a supported bounded alternative without inventing a dynamic grant mechanism. Also reconcile “operator only” heater control with the later agent act after authorization: an approval entry alone does not grant that agent grow.heat. Say which member signs the actual heater command, or explicitly defer the bounded delegated mechanism. Finally, the two requested capabilities cover sensor reads and file custody, not actuator writes. Explain whether the story only records simulated/desired commands or relies on a future bounded device-command owner with recorded outcomes. A successful state transition must not stand for an established physical device change. The application guide’s outside-effect/unknown-outcome boundary applies.\n\nF3 (P2), lines 323–328: no keys in a sandbox does not prove that the custom heatmap “can do nothing else” beyond its listed reads/navigation. Section 5.1 of the cited applications note allows a package frame to ask its host for reads and signing; the host owns and enforces projection, installation, scope and act boundaries. State that enforced boundary here. If the surface is intended to be read-only apart from navigation, name that as a host-enforced future permission rather than infer it from absence of keys. Keep the room-act/signing prohibition and M1 trusted-package limit intact.\n\nF4 (P2), lines 246–248: distinguish reporting a device observation from changing the device item. A current comment-form act has no guards, effects or sends; validate/handlers.ts at reviewed Main explicitly rejects them. If observe only records a summary/reported state in its input, say so and explain the projection; if it changes a folded device item's state, use a supported guarded transition or a separate transition. Do not describe a comment as performing that item-state change. Link the relevant current form/validator and ten terms.\n\nF5 (P2), lines 359–365: remove the unsupported promise that readings cannot crowd out acts because both are few. Whole-scope admission bounds retained Used plus the reservations of already admitted work. It does not reserve space for every future ordinary operator act. A finite daily rate still eventually fills finite retained-entry/byte budgets, and new work can then be refused. Describe the desired sampling/retention or admission policy as future work, and distinguish funded pending settlements from unadmitted new acts; do not claim a capacity guarantee from traffic estimates. Capacity remains under review and its R4 values are provisional.\n\nF6 (P3), line 309: the example context always says “open”. The same alert becomes held at its next revision in the stated story, so that template would send the model an incorrect state even while action visibility checks state. Make the example derive its state/waiting status from the displayed revision, or explicitly restrict that example to an open-only view and describe the held view. This asks for a truthful sketch, not adoption of a runtime schema.\n\nArchitecture conclusion: the generic card/heatmap and separate site/room scopes are a useful destination sketch, but milestone dependencies, supported act forms and the actuator path need the corrections above. Security conclusion: the authority table and sandbox explanation do not yet establish their claimed boundaries (F2/F3); an authorization record, a view button or absence of frame keys must not substitute for enforced signing/grant/capability rules. Simplification conclusion: keep the shared kit and explicit sketch; use the existing fixed-action/act and host boundary concepts, and avoid hiding additional M2/device-owner work inside this note.\n\nCoverage and provenance: primary f63e1f3d read FIRST and in full. The actual file is 432 lines, rather than the request's 330-line routing count. Its single artifact is fresh/unretired and paging is exhausted. Read the current Main ten terms and application guide, relevant applications-note principles/package/trust/milestone sections, the MVP order, ActType and validator clauses, and checked against the already reviewed card design and capacity contract. All four explicit Markdown links resolve at Main, including the archive anchor. Add navigable citations for the ten terms, plan 032 and the current-source rules supporting these claims, rather than relying only on their names in the final Source paragraph. The prototype observations remain planner-attributed references; I did not access private prototype code or claim to reproduce its host behavior. The note names no product, clearly calls the view form a sketch, and commissions no implementation; preserve those limits.\n\nDocument-only verification: Main df806354d7008f9d7d961c92d5f87c7fc97e6fc9 has tree 15aa1842152c86b1d60277f810028c9484c50624. Independently compared all 1980 Main leaves: every blob and mode is unchanged, with only this new note giving 1981 leaves. Source and tests are unchanged. Whitespace check passes. Per docs/testing.md's document-only rule, no test suite or gate was rerun. No diagram, scenario, runtime, phone, multi-user, device, custody or host acceptance is claimed by this review. No landing/push/deployment authorization for this rejected head. Return a repaired exact Source for the remaining review.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:f63e1f3d22cd4d42a6e0b2400c311eae19e94bb2","binding":"assigned","head":"96d82e16ab8337050031a3dcd1f0e449ea409900","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3fede3620135a4e6cc317341e09b243a41914816\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:00fa3165e633246022d5ef040a093d813150b72d\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:48a603e432bf660b10bdb41f37852e8d2dd0e60f","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}